u often repeat this idea we've got to  balance the budget I'm a deficit Hawk  we've got to reduce the deficit until a  Republican is president then you often  forget about it but if absent any  context  your priority is we've got a cut cut cut  to reduce the deficit you can't deal  with the social issues that you claim to  be liberal on you just can't do it so  very often when you hear someone say I'm  a centrist or I'm a social liberal and  fiscal conservative you're really  dealing with what's called the  right-wing libertarian these are  essentially right-wingers who don't hate  gay people right I mean this is a gross  oversimplification but when I hear  socially liberal fiscally conservative  in practice I hear I'm a right winger  but I'm not interested in persecuting  gay people another way to understand  socially liberal and fiscally  conservative is I really would love to  fix the issues that are affecting the  poor marginalized often minority  disenfranchised individuals but I don't  want the government to get involved and  definitely none of my tax money should  go towards fixing those problems it's  not a very principled or coherent  position that's the reality if you  support these social rights you have to  have an economy that's going to provide  and guarantee those rights otherwise  it's just nonsense there's a lot of  corporate CEOs that want to be relevant  that take this approach sometimes they  call it centrism  Starbucks CEO Howard Schultz is sort of  one of those guys he's a social  progressive about non discrimination in  the workplace he's for gay marriage all  of that stuff but economically he's  essentially a standard neoliberal who is  about free markets he's suspicious of  caricatures of socialism that float  around all of that stuff now the other  thing I will say is it's a very small  group of people who actually are  socially liberal and fiscally  conservative not when you say hey where  do you fall on the political spectrum  when you do